### The error contract — RFC 9457 application/problem+json
### Every error is a problem+json document with a stable `type` URI, `title`, `status`, `detail`,
### `instance`, and (for validation) a machine-readable `errors[]`. See docs/ERROR_CATALOG.md.
### Mirrors the security + functional integration tests.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000

### 401 — no credentials
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/appointments

### 401 — a tampered/garbage bearer token
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/appointments
Authorization: Bearer not.a.real.jwt

### Log in as a patient for the authorization cases below
# @name patientLogin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "patient@aurora-clinic.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

### 403 — a patient calling an admin-only endpoint
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/clinics
Authorization: Bearer {{patientLogin.response.body.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rogue Clinic",
  "timezone": "America/Los_Angeles",
  "address": "1 Nowhere St",
  "cancellation_cutoff_hours": 24
}

### 404 — an appointment id that does not exist (well-formed uuid)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/appointments/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{patientLogin.response.body.access_token}}

### 422 — validation error with a field-level errors[] array (bad email, missing password)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "not-an-email"
}

### 422 — a reserved/special-use email TLD is rejected by EmailStr (e.g. .test)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "someone@example.test",
  "password": "password123"
}

### For 409 (illegal state transition) and 412 (stale ETag), see appointments.http —
### they need a booked appointment to act on.
